              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your patient, a 33-year-old male, who presented on 30/11/2015 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically suspicious for appendicitis. Physical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Blood tests showed elevated inflammatory markers with leukocytosis. Abdominal ultrasonography confirmed acute appendicitis without evidence of perforation. The patient underwent a laparoscopic emergency appendectomy on 01/12/2015 without intraoperative complications. Postoperatively, he received intravenous fluid replacement, analgesic medication, and antibiotic therapy. Wound care was performed and vital signs remained stable throughout. The patient recovered well and was discharged on 03/12/2015 in good general condition. Please ensure follow-up in approximately two weeks for wound inspection and review of pathology results. Do not hesitate to contact me with any questions.</div>"
            },
